.TH CHTTPSVR_START 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_start \- register a listener with the running engine
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_start(chttpsvr srv,
                             const chttpsvr_config_t *cfg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Starts the server on the port and the host that cfg names.  cfg may be
NULL, and the server then uses the defaults of
CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT.

cfg->host names the TCP listener, with the rules of the listener of
the Go net package:

  NULL or ""     (NULL is the default of CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT)
                 every interface, with one dual-stack socket on [::]
                 (IPV6_V6ONLY off) that also takes IPv4 clients through
                 v4-mapped addresses; 0.0.0.0 on a host without IPv6.
                 cfg->ipv6_only makes it an IPv6-only listener.
  an address     exactly that address: "0.0.0.0" is every IPv4
                 interface, "::" every IPv6 interface, "127.0.0.1" the
                 IPv4 loopback alone.  An address in brackets, as in
                 "[::1]", is the address inside the brackets; a name
                 in brackets fails the start.
  a host name    its first IPv4 address when it has one, else its first
                 IPv6 address; "localhost" listens on 127.0.0.1.  With
                 cfg->ipv6_only, its first IPv6 address when it has
                 one, else its first IPv4 address.

The server tries that one address alone.  When it cannot bind it, for
example because another socket holds the port on that address, the
start fails with ccol_unexpected_failure, and the server never listens
on another address of the same name in its place.  A host name that
does not resolve, or a malformed bracketed address, fails the start in
the same way, and the engine logger reports the reason.

cfg->host can also be a "unix://path/to/socket" string.  The server
then binds a Unix domain socket at that path, and ignores cfg->port.
The server replaces a socket file at that path when no process listens
on it.  The start fails with ccol_unexpected_failure, and does not
change the file, in these cases:
  - The path gives an object that is not a socket.
  - The path gives a socket on which a live server listens.
chttpsvr_stop and the destroy remove the socket file only while the
path gives the socket that this server created.  They get to the file
through the directory that held it at the start.  A later chdir() therefore
has no effect.  One chttpsvr instance listens on TCP or on a Unix
socket, never on both.  To use both, create two instances.

An empty path fails the start with ccol_unexpected_failure.  A path
that does not fit in the sun_path field of struct sockaddr_un also
fails the start with ccol_unexpected_failure.  That field holds 108
bytes on Linux and 104 bytes on the BSDs, with the terminating NUL.
The server resolves a relative path against the working directory at
the time of the start.

This call starts the shared ccol_event_loop reactor of the process,
and the idle-timeout sweep thread beside it, when they do not run yet.
It then blocks until the library confirms that they run, and only then
continues.  This reactor is separate from the engine of chttpclient.
A start or a stop of one has no effect on the other.  The function
then binds the listener socket synchronously.  It returns after the
socket is in the LISTEN state and registered with the reactor.

You can start more than one chttpsvr handle at the same time, on
different ports or on different Unix socket paths.  All of them share
the same reactor.

The function rejects a port of 0 with ccol_invalid_args.  It accepts
it only when cfg->host is a "unix://" path, where the port has no
meaning and the server ignores it.

The server creates a ctpool at start time, and it dispatches every
buffered handler through that pool.  The pool has
cfg->worker_thread_count threads.  Its queue capacity is
cfg->worker_queue_capacity, where 0 means 1024 slots for each thread,
and CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED means no limit on the capacity.
Streaming handlers run on a second pool of their own, which the server
creates at the first streaming request that it receives; see "Slow
clients" below.  cfg->streaming_thread_count set to
CHTTPSVR_STREAMING_POOL_OFF runs them on the worker pool instead.  The
server owns both pools, and destroys them when something destroys the
server.

Rejection of a route: the server rejects a route that does not match,
or that is malformed, with 404, 405 or 500.  It decides this before it
reads any body byte.  It rejects a matched route whose ctpool is at
capacity with 503 and a "Retry-After: 5" header.  It answers both with a
courtesy error response, and a small dedicated pool of its own runs
any middleware of that rejection.  No thread ever waits for the client
to read that response: a write that meets a full socket parks the
connection, with no thread, until the client reads, and the internal
ceiling of 2 seconds (see max_response_write_duration_us) ends it.  A
client that reads slowly and gets a "no" therefore cannot hold a
thread, nor delay the dispatch of any other, unrelated connection.
The interim "100 Continue" line of a buffered route parks in the same
way, and the body is read once the line is out.

Lingering close: when the server closes a connection after a complete
response while the client can still be sending (the unread body of a
refused request, a body that a handler left unread, or pipelined
bytes), a plain close would make the kernel answer with a reset, which
can destroy the response before the client reads it.  The server
instead sends a TLS close_notify when the connection uses TLS, shuts
down its side of the connection for writing, and reads and discards
what the client still sends, for at most 2 seconds, before it closes.
The client therefore reads the whole response and then an orderly end
of the stream, and so does a client that sends the whole body of a
refused upload before it reads the response, as long as it finishes
sending within that time.  A lingering connection holds no
thread, and it counts against cfg->max_connections until it closes.  A
refused request whose framing has no body, with nothing more in the
socket, closes at once.

Limit on the body size: cfg->max_body_size caps the request body, and
0 means unlimited.  The server rejects a buffered route whose body is
above this with 413, before the handler runs.  The server always calls
the handler of a streaming route.  For a streaming route,
chttpsvr_req_read() returns -1 and chttpsvr_req_stream_error() gives
ccol_msg_too_large after the body crosses the limit.  In both cases
the server closes the connection after the response (Connection:
close), and does not keep it alive.  It does this because it does not
read the body bytes above the limit.  That close is a lingering close;
see "Lingering close" above.

The server rejects a buffered route whose declared Content-Length is
already above max_body_size with 413 at once.  It does not wait for a
body byte first, and a stalled or malicious peer may never send one.
This does not apply to a streaming route.  The server always calls the
handler of a streaming route, whatever the declared Content-Length is,
which matches the contract above.  The route kind does not matter for
one more check.  The server also rejects any single chunk of a chunked
Transfer-Encoding whose declared size is above max_body_size.  It does
so as soon as it parses the chunk-size line of that chunk, and before
it waits for the data of that chunk.  This appears in the same way as
any other malformed body for that route kind, that is, as a 413 or as
ccol_msg_too_large.

Every timeout and duration of chttpsvr_config_t is a uint64_t count of
microseconds, rounded up to whole milliseconds inside the server; see
CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT(3).

Timeouts for a body read: cfg->stream_read_timeout_us bounds the gap
between two batches of body bytes.  It applies to a buffered body and
to a streaming body.  A value of 0 waits forever.  It resets on every
new byte, so a client that sends its data just fast enough never trips
it.  cfg->max_body_read_duration_us therefore
separately bounds the *total* time that the server spends to read the
body of one request, whatever the progress in each gap is.  Its
default is 300000000, that is, 5 minutes, and 0 turns it off.  See
chttpsvr_req_read.  cfg->response_write_timeout_us bounds the
equivalent gap on the response-write side, and 0 there means the value
of stream_read_timeout_us.  It bounds how long a response can wait on
a client that stops reading; the send queue of the socket shrinking
counts as progress.  See CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT(3).
cfg->max_response_write_duration_us is the write-side equivalent of
max_body_read_duration_us.  Its default is 300000000, that is, 5 minutes,
and 0 turns it off.  It separately bounds the *total* time that the
server spends to send one response, whatever the progress in each call
is.  After it expires, the send of the response fails and the server
closes the connection.

For a buffered route no thread waits out any of these four limits: a
body that stops arriving, or a response that meets a full socket,
parks its connection with no thread (see "Slow clients" below), and
the limits then apply to the parked connection.  The idle-timeout
sweep thread checks them once a second, and the server checks them
again when the connection becomes ready, so a client that stalls past
a limit and then comes back meets the same answer.  An expired body
gets 408 Request Timeout and a close, and an expired response is cut
off and closed.  A streaming handler waits on its own thread inside
chttpsvr_req_read(), and there the limits bound that wait directly.

Slow clients: a worker thread never waits on the socket of a client
for a buffered route.  When the body of a request stops arriving, the
worker parks the connection, which then holds no thread, and returns
to the pool.  When more of the body is ready, the reactor hands the
connection to any free worker, which continues where the other one
stopped.  A response write that meets a full socket parks the
connection in the same way, and a later worker writes the rest.
Keep-alive, pipelining, "Expect: 100-continue", HEAD, 204 and 304 work
across a park exactly as without one.  A parked body asks the kernel
to wake the reactor only once the rest of the body, or 16 KiB of it,
is queued (SO_RCVLOWAT), so that a client that sends a byte at a time
costs one wake-up and not one for each byte.

cfg->min_transfer_rate_bps sets a minimum average rate, 240 bytes a
second by default, for the body of a request and for the response to
it.  The server measures it from the start of the body read, or of the
response write, and judges it once cfg->min_transfer_rate_grace_us,
5000000 (5 s) by default, has passed.  A body below the floor gets 408
Request Timeout and a close; a response below it is cut off and
closed; a streaming handler sees chttpsvr_req_read() fail with
chttpsvr_req_stream_error() giving ccol_timed_out.  Time that the
server itself holds a request back, in a wait for body memory or in
the streaming queue, never counts against the client.
CHTTPSVR_NO_RATE_FLOOR turns the floor off.

cfg->max_partial_body_memory, 256 MiB by default, caps the memory that
the bodies of buffered requests hold together on this server, from the
first byte that the server reads until the handler returns.  A body
with a Content-Length reserves its whole
length before the server reads a byte of it.  When it does not fit,
the request waits with no thread, the server reads nothing from it,
and it sends no "100 Continue" until memory frees.  A chunked body
reserves its buffer as the buffer grows, the old buffer included
while a growth copies it, and waits in the same way when it runs out.
The buffer of a body with a Content-Length takes exactly that length,
so a reservation always covers the memory that the body holds.
Waiting requests are admitted strictly in the order in which they
began to wait, so a large body is never starved by small ones.  When
every body that holds memory is itself waiting for more, the oldest
waiter goes ahead past the limit, one request at a time, so the limit
is exceeded by less than twice max_body_size and the server never
deadlocks.  The server frees a body, and gives its reservation back,
as soon as its handler returns, before the response goes out, so a
response that waits for a slow reader holds no body; a request that is
refused, cut off or closed frees both at once.  A body whose handler
runs is not waiting, so a request that a handler waits for, and whose
body does not fit beside the bodies that running handlers hold, waits
until its deadline.  A request that waits longer than cfg->body_memory_wait_timeout_us, 30000000 (30 s)
by default, gets 503 Service Unavailable with "Retry-After: 5" and a
close.  A body that arrives complete together with its headers, and
every streaming route, reserves nothing.  CHTTPSVR_NO_MEMORY_CAP turns
the limit off, and CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE removes the deadline of the
wait.

Streaming handlers run on their own pool of
cfg->streaming_thread_count threads, the worker thread count by
default, so that slow streaming clients can occupy at most that pool
and never the threads that serve buffered routes.  A streaming request
that finds no free streaming thread waits, with no thread, in a queue
of cfg->streaming_queue_capacity entries, four times the streaming
thread count by default.  A request that finds the queue full gets 503
with "Retry-After: 5" at once, and one that waits longer than
cfg->streaming_queue_timeout_us, 5000000 (5 s) by default, gets the same
answer.  A handler never runs for a request that got that 503.

Every limit here reads 0 as its default, so a configuration that
never mentions a field keeps the defense.  Each one turns off only
through its own named constant, from <ccollections/chttpserver.h>:

  CHTTPSVR_NO_RATE_FLOOR       ((unsigned)-1), for min_transfer_rate_bps:
                               no minimum rate; a body read or a
                               response write is then bounded only by
                               stream_read_timeout_us,
                               max_body_read_duration_us,
                               response_write_timeout_us and
                               max_response_write_duration_us.
  CHTTPSVR_NO_MEMORY_CAP       ((size_t)-1), for max_partial_body_memory:
                               no limit on body memory, so no request
                               waits for it.
  CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE         UINT64_MAX, for body_memory_wait_timeout_us
                               and streaming_queue_timeout_us: the wait
                               lasts as long as it takes.  It holds no
                               thread, and a destroy of the server still
                               ends it.  Every other value, however
                               large, is a finite deadline.
  CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED     ((size_t)-1), for worker_queue_capacity and
                               streaming_queue_capacity: no limit on the
                               queue, so no request gets a 503 because the
                               queue is full.
  CHTTPSVR_STREAMING_POOL_OFF  (-1), for streaming_thread_count:
                               streaming handlers run on the worker
                               pool, with no pool and no queue of their
                               own.  worker_queue_capacity then bounds
                               them, and a slow streaming client can
                               hold a worker thread for as long as the
                               read limits allow.

The 503 that the server writes itself always carries "Retry-After: 5".

Idle timeout of a connection: the idle-timeout sweep thread of this
module applies cfg->read_timeout_us and cfg->idle_timeout_us.  Every
chttpsvr instance in the process shares that thread.  No OS timer for
each connection applies these two fields.  idle_timeout_us wins over
read_timeout_us when idle_timeout_us is above 0.  The server uses
read_timeout_us otherwise.  A value of 0 in both turns the idle
timeout off completely.  This only bounds the time that a connection
spends idle between requests, while it waits for the next pipelined or
keep-alive request.  It never bounds the time that a worker thread
spends to handle a request.

Limit on the duration of a header read:
cfg->max_header_read_duration_us bounds the *total* wall-clock time
that one connection can spend in the reactor-owned phase of one
request.  That phase is the TLS handshake, where there is one, plus
the read of the whole header block of that request.  Its default is
30000000, that is, 30 s, and 0 turns it off.  read_timeout_us and
idle_timeout_us above reset on every byte that arrives.  A peer that
sends its request headers, or its handshake, one byte at a time, and
never completes them, therefore defeats both of them.  Such a peer
never comes near max_header_bytes either.  This field is the
header-phase equivalent of max_body_read_duration_us.  The same
idle-timeout sweep thread applies it.  That sweep runs once a second
and closes every connection that expired, however many there are, so a
connection lives up to about a second longer than its deadline.  See
CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT(3).  The budget is for each
request.  It starts at the first handshake step, or at the first byte
of the header block of the request when there is no handshake.  The
next request of a keep-alive connection starts a fresh budget when its
own first byte arrives.  After the budget expires, the server closes
the connection and sends no response, because the header block was
never complete enough to answer.

Limit on the header size: cfg->max_header_bytes bounds the combined
size of the header block of one request, and 0 means the library
default of 64 KiB.  The server answers a request whose headers are
above this with 400 Bad Request and then closes the connection.  The
request parser of this module reports every other malformed request
before the routing in the same way.  The answer is best effort: a peer
that is still streaming its oversized header block when the server
closes can meet a reset before it reads anything.  Whatever this
value, the request line and each header line must end within 8 KiB,
CRLF included, and a request holds at most 100 header lines.

Request validation: the server refuses these requests before any
route, middleware or handler sees them, and closes the connection
after the answer.  A request line whose HTTP version is not 1.x, such
as HTTP/0.9, HTTP/2.0 or HTTP/3.0, gets 505 HTTP Version Not Supported;
a higher minor version of HTTP/1 is served as HTTP/1.1 (RFC 9110
section 2.5).  A Host header outside the uri-host [ ":" port ] grammar
of RFC 9112 section 3.2 gets 400: the host must be a reg-name of
unreserved, sub-delims and percent-encoded bytes, an IPv4 address, or
a bracketed IP literal, so a '/', '@', '?', '#' or whitespace, an
empty host, or a port that is not all digits is refused.  A
Transfer-Encoding that names a coding other than chunked, such as
"gzip, chunked", gets 501 Not Implemented, because the server decodes
no other coding; one that does not end in chunked gets 400.  The chunk
framing of a body (size lines, chunk extensions and CRLFs) may exceed
16 bytes plus twice the data of each chunk by 16 KiB in all; a body
past that is malformed, so a buffered route answers 400 and
chttpsvr_req_read of a streaming route fails with
ccol_http_transfer_aborted.  An HTTP/1.0 request that carries
"Expect: 100-continue" gets no interim 100 response (RFC 9110 section
10.1.1).

Request target: the server accepts an origin-form target, which is a
path that begins with "/", and an absolute-form target with the scheme
http or https, whose path it serves and whose authority it ignores.
An absolute-form target with an empty path stands for "/".  Every
other target gets 400 Bad Request and a close, before the route search
and before any middleware runs.  That covers a path with no leading
"/", a bare query such as "?x", and any other scheme.

The asterisk form follows Go's net/http server exactly.  The server
answers "OPTIONS *" itself with 200 OK and a Content-Length of 0, and
runs no route, no middleware and no handler.  The response carries the
same headers as any other response, date, content-length and
connection, and no Allow header.  The connection follows the ordinary
keep-alive rules.
The server reads and discards a request body of up to 4 KiB.  A larger
body, or one that fails to arrive, still gets the 200, and the
connection closes after it.  A declared Content-Length above 4 KiB is
not read at all, and no 100 Continue invites it.  Any other method with
the target "*" gets 400 Bad Request and a close, a method outside the
seven chttp_method_t names included, such as "PROPFIND *".  Such a
method with any other target gets 501 Not Implemented.

Date: each final response has a Date header with the current time in
UTC, in the IMF-fixdate form of RFC 9110 section 5.6.7.  The exception
is a handler that set its own Date with chttpsvr_resp_set_header(3).
This rule applies to handler responses, HEAD, each rejection that the
server writes itself, "OPTIONS *", and each response on a keep-alive
connection.  A 1xx response and the interim 100 Continue have no Date,
as in the net/http server of Go.  The headers that the server adds
itself are therefore:
  - date.
  - content-length.  The server does not add it to a 1xx, a 204, a
    304, or a HEAD response for which the handler wrote no body.
  - connection.
  - allow on a 405, and retry-after on a 503 that the server writes
    itself.

Host: a request with more than one Host header gets 400 Bad Request,
for all versions.  An HTTP/1.1 request with no Host header also gets
400 Bad Request.  An HTTP/1.0 request does not need a Host header.
With two Host lines, a front-end can forward one authority while this
server reads a different authority.

The request target must not hold whitespace (a space or a horizontal
tab), other control characters or DEL.  A request line that does not
obey this rule gets 400 Bad Request and a close, before the routing.

Framing: the server reads Transfer-Encoding as one list.  It merges
that list from all Transfer-Encoding lines.  These rules apply:
  - chunked must be the last coding of that list.
  - chunked must occur in the list one time at most.
  - The server refuses a Transfer-Encoding together with a
    Content-Length.
A request that does not obey one of these rules gets 400 Bad Request
and a close, before the routing.

A malformed body is a client error.  It gets 400, not 500.  These
bodies are malformed:
  - A body with a chunk size that is not hexadecimal.
  - A body with a chunk that has no CRLF at its end.
  - A body with a malformed trailer line.
  - A body that ends before the length that its framing gives.
A body above max_body_size gets 413, not 400.  A body that stops
arriving gets 408, not 400.

An HTTP/1.0 request that has Transfer-Encoding gets 400 Bad Request and
a close, whatever its Connection header says, as RFC 9112 section 6.1
requires.  A chunk-size line must obey the full chunk-extension grammar
of RFC 9112 section 7.1.1.  A control byte in an extension makes the
body malformed, and the request gets 400.  A "close" token in a
Connection header closes the connection after the response, for all
HTTP versions.  This is also true when the header also has a
"keep-alive" token.

Limit on the connection count: cfg->max_connections bounds how many
connections this listener can have open at one time, and 0 means
unlimited.  At capacity, new connections stay pending in the listen
backlog of the kernel.  The server does not accept them and then
reject them.  The listener itself stops accepting until a slot frees,
and then resumes automatically.  The worst case is about one second
between the free of a slot and the moment that the listener notices
it.

A temporary shortage of resources pauses this listener in the same
way.  Such a shortage happens when the process or the system is
temporarily out of file descriptors, and when a custom allocator from
ccol_create_chttpsvr_mp temporarily cannot serve an allocation for a
connection.  New connections then stay pending until a periodic sweep
resumes the listener, with the same worst case of one second.  This
keeps a temporary shortage on one listener from a delay of any other,
unrelated connection on any other chttpsvr instance that shares the
same reactor thread.

Socket options: the server sets TCP_NODELAY on every TCP connection
that it accepts, and it leaves the socket buffer sizes of such a
connection to the kernel, so that the buffer autotuning of Linux
(tcp(7)) stays on.  On a connection of a Unix domain socket listener,
which has no autotuning, it raises both socket buffers to at least 128
KiB.  The three configurable options below all default to false, the
server applies each one as well as it can, and each one does nothing
on a Unix domain socket listener.  cfg->enable_keepalive sets SO_KEEPALIVE on
every
connection that the server accepts.  cfg->enable_reuseport sets
SO_REUSEPORT on the socket that listens, which lets more than one
chttpsvr instance bind the same host and port, so that the kernel
load-balances the accepts.  cfg->ipv6_only sets IPV6_V6ONLY on an
AF_INET6 listener, so that the listener does not also accept
IPv4-mapped connections.

Restart: you can call chttpsvr_start again on the same handle after a
call to chttpsvr_stop, and that registers a new listener with the new
configuration.  The call returns at once.  Fresh pools replace the
pools of the previous run at once, and the old pools drain in the
background: a request of the previous run that still runs finishes
normally, with its own response, and the restart neither waits for it
nor cuts it off.  A keep-alive connection that stayed open across the
stop is served under the new configuration from its next request on.
The server frees any TLS context of the cycle before that
automatically, before it installs the new one; a connection that still
uses it keeps it until that connection closes.

Do not call chttpsvr_start from a request handler or a middleware of
srv to restart the very server that the handler runs on.  That handler
usually calls chttpsvr_stop first.  The library refuses such a call
with ccol_not_permitted, and does not try it.  The refusal leaves srv in a safe state that you can recover from.
That state is the configuration that srv had before, or a stopped
server when chttpsvr_stop already ran.  A later, legitimate call to
chttpsvr_start from a different thread still restarts it normally.

TLS: cfg->tls, when it is not NULL, must have both cert_path and
key_path set.  The function rejects any other shape with
ccol_invalid_args, before it tries any other work.  It does not
silently start the server as plain, unencrypted HTTP.  Those other
shapes are: exactly one of the two set; cfg->tls->ca_bundle_path set
with neither of the two; and a cfg->tls that is otherwise empty, for
example CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT.  A trust store alone is never valid
server-side configuration, and neither is an otherwise empty
chttp_tls_config_t.  Server-side configuration always needs an
identity certificate for the server.  The client side is different
there: ca_bundle_path alone is the ordinary way to set up verification
against a custom CA with no client certificate at all.

chttpsvr_start fails with ccol_unexpected_failure, and registers no
listener, when cert_path and key_path are both set but the library
cannot load the certificate and key pair.  That load fails for a file
that is missing or unreadable, for a path that does not name a regular
file (a directory, a FIFO or a device, which the library refuses at
once without waiting on it), for a file above the ceiling of 16 MB that
the library reads every PEM artefact under, and for malformed contents.
cfg->tls->ca_bundle_path must also load successfully when you set it,
under the same rules.  That bundle is what turns on mutual TLS on the
resulting listener.  It must also contribute at least one certificate.
A bundle that the library can read but that parses to no certificate,
or to certificate revocation lists alone, is a load failure, and not an
empty trust store.  The function reports a failure to load it as
ccol_unexpected_failure too.  It does not silently start the server
without the verification of client certificates that you asked for.

A ca_bundle_path that holds certificate revocation lists (CRLs) beside
its CA certificates enforces them.  Every certificate of the chain that
a client presents, below its trust anchor, then needs a CRL of its
issuer in the bundle, as the ssl_crl directive of nginx needs.  The
handshake fails for a revoked client certificate, for a certificate
whose issuer has no CRL in the bundle, and for a CRL past its next
update time.  The bundle is read here, so a renewed CRL takes effect at
the next chttpsvr_start.  See CHTTP_TLS_DEFAULT(3).

Mutual TLS requires a client certificate by default.  The handshake of
a client that presents none fails, and so does the handshake of a
client whose certificate does not verify against the bundle.
cfg->tls->client_cert_optional relaxes the first rule only: the server
then also serves a client that presents no certificate, and
chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(3) tells a handler which kind of
client it serves.  chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_der(3),
chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_sha256(3) and chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_subject(3)
tell it which client authenticated.

The listener resumes TLS sessions, from its session cache and from TLS
1.3 tickets, for TLS 1.2 and TLS 1.3, with or without a ca_bundle_path,
and for a client that presents a certificate and one that does not.
Each call of chttpsvr_start builds a new TLS configuration with a new
session cache, so a session from an earlier cycle ends in a full
handshake, and never in a failure.

Pipelining: a client can write its next request before it reads the
previous response.  A keep-alive connection serves such a client, on a
buffered route and on a streaming route.  Bytes of a later request can
arrive in the same read as the headers or the body of the current
request.  The server keeps those bytes for the next request.  It never
drops them.  Pipelined requests can arrive together in one TLS record.
The server answers all of them with no more bytes from the client,
exactly as over plain TCP.

Each response that has a body gives its length in Content-Length.  The
server sends a TLS close_notify before it closes a TLS connection whose
handshake is complete.  Some clients treat a TLS end of stream without
close_notify as a truncation.  Such a client therefore never sees a
truncation from this server.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when srv is CHTTPSVR_INVALID or a stale handle that
something already destroyed; when cfg->port is 0 and cfg->host is not
a "unix://" path; when cfg->tls is set but cert_path and key_path are
not both set; or when cfg->streaming_thread_count is negative and not
CHTTPSVR_STREAMING_POOL_OFF.
ccol_not_permitted when this server already started, or when this call
came from a request handler or a middleware of srv.
ccol_not_enough_memory when a memory allocation fails, or when the
library could not create the shared engine, that is, its reactor
threads or its own diagnostics logger.
ccol_unexpected_failure when the library could not create or bind the
listen socket (for example because another socket holds the port on
the address that cfg->host names, or because cfg->host does not
resolve or is malformed), could not register it with the shared
reactor, or could not start the shared idle-timeout sweep thread.  It also gives this
when cfg->tls was set and the library could not load its certificate
and key pair or its ca_bundle_path.  It also gives this when the
process already holds PTHREAD_KEYS_MAX thread-specific keys, so that
the library cannot create the key that marks the worker threads of a
server.  The server then does not start, and a later call tries again.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The library leaves the disposition of SIGPIPE to the application.  It
never changes it, neither here nor anywhere else.  No write of the
server raises that signal: every write to a connection, a TLS record
and the close_notify of a TLS teardown included, is a send(2) or a
sendmsg(2) with MSG_NOSIGNAL.  A client that closes or resets its
connection while the server writes a response therefore ends that one
connection, and never the process, whatever disposition of SIGPIPE the
application has chosen.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
chttpsvr_stop closes only the listener socket.  A connection that the
server accepted before the stop keeps running.  It can still dispatch
more requests on this same handle at any point during a later restart.
Every such request goes to the pools that the server runs with at that
moment, and a request that runs on a pool of the previous run finishes
there before that pool is destroyed.  A restart is therefore safe to
call while such a connection is still active, and no request ever goes
to a pool that something tears down at the same time.  A parked
connection holds no thread; it stays with the server and resumes on
the new worker pool.  chttpsvr_destroy waits for the requests of every
run, with the bound that CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT(3) describes.

A chttpsvr_start call that restarts srv is also safe in a race with a
concurrent chttpsvr_stop call on the same handle from a different
thread.  This function waits for the teardown of the old listener in
that stop to finish completely, and then binds a new listener on the
same host and port.  It does not race a bind() against a socket that
the stop did not close yet.

chttpsvr_start is also safe in a race with a concurrent engine-wide
teardown.  Such a teardown comes from chttpsvr_engine_stop(), or from
the graceful shutdown of the shared reactor after the destruction of
the last other server that references it.  This function retries
transparently until that teardown finishes.  It does not race it.
.fi
